{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NC9MhkQhvEP4Ffm1QU2VA9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel01.3-uc001-du\n     2→title: \"Report recursive directory disk usage via du\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory to report disk usage. The\n     6→  differential testing harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary\n     7→  (gdu from coreutils) on the same directory on the same filesystem with the same arguments\n     8→  and environment, and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. Block counts\n     9→  are deterministic when both binaries stat the same files on the same filesystem. All tests\n    10→  use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform block-size default variance.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd009-du.yaml) and shared component PRDs\n    16→  (pkg/sys, pkg/format from rel00.0) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential\n    17→  test for cmd/du.\n    18→\n    19→flow:\n    20→  - id: F1\n    21→    step: |\n    22→      The operator runs the Go du binary on a fixture directory with known file sizes and\n    23→      directory structure. Example: ./bin/du -k testdata/du-fixture/\n    24→  - id: F2\n    25→    step: |\n    26→      cmd/du traverses the directory tree using os.ReadDir (or filepath.Walk), calling\n    27→      pkg/sys.Lstat on each entry to obtain st_blocks for block counts and st_dev/st_ino\n    28→      for hard-link deduplication. It accumulates block counts per directory.\n    29→  - id: F3\n    30→    step: |\n    31→      cmd/du writes one line per directory (or per file with -a) in the format\n    32→      \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\". With -h, sizes are formatted via pkg/format.HumanSize.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/du and gdu with\n    36→      the same arguments, environment, and working directory.\n    37→  - id: F5\n    38→    step: |\n    39→      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n    40→      produce identical size values and path listings because they stat the same files on\n    41→      the same filesystem.\n    42→  - id: F6\n    43→    step: |\n    44→      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    45→      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n    46→\n    47→touchpoints:\n    48→  - T1: \"cmd/du — recursive traversal, block accumulation, hard-link dedup, flag modes (prd009-du R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n    49→  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat/Lstat for st_blocks, st_dev, st_ino, InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n    50→  - T3: \"pkg/format — HumanSize for -h output\"\n    51→  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n    52→\n    53→success_criteria:\n    54→  - id: S1\n    55→    criterion: cmd/du compiles and ./bin/du exists after mage build.\n    56→  - id: S2\n    57→    criterion: |\n    58→      Default recursive mode produces one line per subdirectory and the root directory,\n    59→      matching gdu -k output on the same fixture.\n    60→  - id: S3\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      -s summary mode produces one line per argument with the total size, matching gdu -sk\n    63→      output.\n    64→  - id: S4\n    65→    criterion: |\n    66→      -a all-files mode produces one line per file and directory, matching gdu -ak output.\n    67→  - id: S5\n    68→    criterion: |\n    69→      -h human-readable mode produces size strings (K/M/G) that match gdu -h output on the\n    70→      same fixture.\n    71→  - id: S6\n    72→    criterion: |\n    73→      -c grand total mode produces a \"total\" line after all arguments, matching gdu -ck output.\n    74→  - id: S7\n    75→    criterion: |\n    76→      Hard-link deduplication works: a fixture with two directory entries pointing to the same\n    77→      inode produces a total that counts the file's blocks only once, matching gdu behavior.\n    78→  - id: S8\n    79→    criterion: |\n    80→      --apparent-size mode uses st_size instead of st_blocks, matching gdu --apparent-size\n    81→      output.\n    82→  - id: S9\n    83→    criterion: |\n    84→      The differential test passes for all flag combinations: default -k, -sk, -ak, -h,\n    85→      -ck, -d 1, --apparent-size, and missing path.\n    86→\n    87→out_of_scope:\n    88→  - Cross-filesystem behavior (files on different mount points) is not tested in the\n    89→    differential harness; both binaries see the same single filesystem.\n    90→  - SIGPIPE handling is specified in prd009-du R5.1 but is not directly testable in the\n    91→    differential harness.\n    92→  - Sparse file creation depends on the filesystem; --apparent-size tests use a regular file\n    93→    with known size rather than a true sparse file.\n    94→\n    95→test_suite: test-rel01.3\n    96→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYkzfVfiafWLqmfhdyEAb4","session_id":"ffcf16cc-f1bd-4af0-8cf7-4be580f5f5ef","uuid":"229152b0-c64a-4f39-a957-8fc26627f791"}
